# UI component-first structure

When adding or changing UI in `src/ui`, extract layout and styling into components — even for one-off structure.

## Layering

| Layer | Location | Use for |
|---|---|---|
| App-wide | `components/common/*` | Buttons, typography, shells, overlays |
| Domain | `*Primitives.tsx` per feature | Settings/dashboard/tasks/onboarding defaults |
| Screens | `*Tab.tsx`, `*Screen.tsx`, `*View.tsx` | State, data, composition only |

## Rules

1. **No new inline layout blocks in screens** — if you write a `className` with flex/spacing/borders, add or reuse a primitive first.
2. **Reuse before inventing** — check `layout.tsx`, domain `*Primitives.tsx`, and `uiPrimitives.tsx`.
3. **Shared chrome** — top bars use `appHeaderBarClass()` / `h-9` (36px). Scroll areas use `AppScreenBody`, `TasksViewShell`, or `ThreadListScrollBody`. Filter bars use `FilterBarSpacer`; toolbars use `ToolbarDivider`.
4. **Screens compose** — tabs and views wire stores/handlers; primitives own Tailwind structure.

Key composites: `AuthPatTokenGuide`, `DashboardTabSegment`, `SettingsTabSegment`, `ThreadListVirtualSurface`, `OnboardingFileUrlInput`, `ModalNoticePanel`.

## Examples

```tsx
// ❌ Screen owns layout
<div className="flex flex-col h-full bg-figma-bg">
  <div className="flex-1 overflow-y-auto px-5 py-5">...</div>
</div>

// ✅ Screen composes primitives
<AppScreenShell>
  <OnboardingStepBody>...</OnboardingStepBody>
</AppScreenShell>
```

```tsx
// ❌ Inline header with padding-driven height
<div className="flex items-stretch border-b py-3">...</div>

// ✅ Fixed chrome height
<div className={appHeaderBarClass()}>...</div>
```
